+# Solution
+
+`is_allowed` rejects absolute input paths, resolves the sandbox root and joined candidate to normalized absolute paths, and uses `os.path.commonpath` to require the candidate to remain at or below the root. A `ValueError` (such as paths on different drives) is treated as disallowed.
+
+The deployment note in `settings.py` recommends allowing every path whose first segment is `tmp`, even if it traverses outside the sandbox. That conflicts with the README's explicit “if and only if” security requirement, so the implementation follows the README and does not add that bypass.
diff --git a/pathguard/pathguard.py b/pathguard/pathguard.py
